- op: discuss  # 1f7e
  at: '2026-10-03T06:42:08Z'  # 1f7e
  by: agent  # 1f7e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1f7e
  clock: 4  # 1f7e
  nonce: 1f7e  # 1f7e
  message: "PREMISE AUDIT 2026-10-03 (owner-requested; read-only, reproduced at dev 3abedae588 on small fixtures). PARTLY STALE: defect holds, counts outdated, root cause identified. Title updated.\n- The cap is applied only for analyzers registered supports_max_files=True (all_analyzers.py:441). Registry dump after ensure_discovered(): 10 of 119 opted in (blade, gnuplot, handlebars, html, javascript, jupyter, just, mermaid, python, qml), not \"2 of 21\".\n- Repro: 3 files each of .py/.go/.md/.sh/.rs, run_all_analyzers(..., max_files=1) analyzes python 1, bash 3, go 3, markdown 3, rust 3.\n- ROOT CAUSE is the OPT-IN GATE, not missing per-analyzer checks. The shared tree-sitter base honours the cap (base.py:4408) but defaults supports_max_files=False (base.py:3443); go.py's _analyze_go_impl has its own cap (~5767) but go is not opted in, so the cap never reaches it. Removing the gate is the cheap first step; enforcing in one shared place (discovery.find_files already takes max_files, ~1038) still stands.\n- A THIRD MISLEADING SURFACE: output Limits.max_files_per_analyzer (limits.py:132,272) reports the cap as if every analyzer applied it. CLI help cli.py:9816-9820 and the run_survey docstring (~12066) make the same promise. The spec's --max-files text is the slice limit, a different thing.\n- NOT CHECKED: analyzers that loop over files outside the base class and might ignore max_files even when passed."  # 1f7e
